<title>Solving single autonomous differential equations using graphical methods</title>
<p><ref>Video</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref> </p>
<description>
Using graphical methods, one can observe where the rate of change is positive or negative and determine the behavior of a solution to a differential equation.
</description>




<section><title>Transcript</title>

<section><title>Solving single autonomous differential equations using graphical methods</title>
<p>Let’s say we had the autonomous differential equation dx/dt = f(x), for some function f, with the initial condition, x at time t_0 is some number x_0. Here t_0 could be zero, if we wanted the initial time to be 0, but it could be another time, maybe we’re starting at time 17, then t_0 would be 17. Here we’ll show how to understand the behavior of this differential equation, using a graphical approach. It turns out we can determine the essential behavior of the solution, x(t), without doing any analytic calculations. In fact, we can figure out this behavior just from the graph of the function f(x).</p>
  
<p>From this graph, we can determine what x(t) will do from any initial condition x_0. How can we do this? Well, the derivative dx/dt is the rate of change of x(t). If dx/dt is large and positive, then we know x is increasing at a fast rate. If it’s negative, this means x_t is decreasing, and if it’s 0, this means x has zero velocity. How can we graphically determine whether dx/dt is large, positive or negative or zero? Well, dx/dt is just f(x). We can look at the graph of f(x), and just look up the velocity of x. So given a value of the state variable x(t), we can look up f(x). Plug in that value to the function f, and just by looking at the graph, we can see if f is large and positive, then we know that x should be rapidly increasing at that point. On the other hand, if f is negative at that point, then we know x should be decreasing.</p>
  
<p>So for example, if we find that f(x(t))is positive, then we know x(t) is increasing, so we know that for later time, x(t) should move to a larger value. And we can continue this process, looking at what happens to x(t) as t continues to get larger. As it turns out, this ends up being a fairly simple process. Let’s demonstrate this with an example. Let’s look at the differential equation dx/dt = x^2 - 4. We can determine the dynamics of the solution x(t), for any initial condition, simply by looking at the graph of f(x) = x^2 - 4. By looking at the graph of f, we can immediately make some observations.</p>
  
<p>First of all, f(x) is negative in the interval from -2 to 2. So for x in the interval -2 to 2, the rate of change dx/dt must be negative, which implies that x(t) is decreasing. In fact, if x(t) happened to be 0, the speed of this negative change would be greatest, because at least if I drew the figure right, the minimum of the function would occur at x = 0. Now, it's important that on this graph I’m plotting x on the horizontal axis, so if I start off with x = 0, I’m over here, and I can read off that my velocity should be negative. This means that as time increases, x should decrease, so I should move this direction. If at some other time, x were over here at, let’s say negative one and a half, I can again read off my velocity, and see that the velocity should be negative, but the velocity is much lower here than it was when x was 0. Or if x happened to be 1.5, again the velocity is negative, since f(x) is negative when x is 1.5, so x should be decreasing. I should be moving in this direction, but I’m moving more slowly here than I was when x was 0. As long as I start anywhere from -2 to 2, I know that x is decreasing, because f is negative. So if I start over here, at lets say 1.5, with time I’ll move to the left, with smaller and smaller values of x, eventually x will move down to 0, which means it’ll be moving as fast as possible, but it’s still becoming negative, gets down to negative ½, I’ll hit negative 1 eventually, but I still have to keep moving to the left because f is negative. As I get closer and closer to negative 2, my velocity slows down because f is still negative, but it gets pretty small.</p>
  
<p>Now if I were to reach x = -2, what’s my velocity? Well, f(-2) is 0, which means that if x(t) were equal to -2, the velocity would be 0. So if x starts in the interval -2 to 2, it’s going to continue to decrease and get closer and closer to negative 2. And as it gets closer and closer to -2, its velocity gets closer and closer to 0. And in fact, it can never cross -2, because the velocity is 0 there. So what must happen to x(t) as t gets large? It must get closer and closer to -2. So what we’ve concluded is that if x(t) starts in the interval -2 to 2, it must get closer to -2 as t gets large.</p>
  
<p>We can illustrate this result with a plot of x versus t. Notice that in the second plot, I’m using the opposite convention as in the first on the right. Here, x is plotted on the vertical axis, so this point 1 corresponds to the point over here when x is 1, here’s x = 2, negative 1, and negative 2. The special point, x = -2, where we’re heading, corresponds to x = -2 here, which I’m going to draw as a line for all time, we care about that point.</p>
  
<p>So let’s imagine we started with the initial condition, x(0) = 1.5. So when time is 0, I should start at 1.5 over here. And by looking at 1.5 on the graph, we see that f is negative, so x must decrease, so as time increases, as I move to the right on the second graph on the left, x should go down, as I’m moving to the left over here. And in fact as x goes down, f gets more and more negative, so we should speed up. The slope should actually get larger and larger as x gets closer to 0 where the maximum slope occurs. But then once we cross 0 and get to negative numbers, dx/dt is still negative, f is still negative, but now x slows down because f isn’t quite so negative. And as we get closer and closer to negative 2 we slow down, because f approaches 0. Now we’re down over here, we have a small value of f, and as time increases we get closer and closer to the special value of x = -2.  So this green curve is a plot of x(t), for the initial condition x(0) = 1.5.</p>
  
<p>Now what would happen if we chose the initial condition x(0) = -3? Well that means we should start off here at -3, where f is large and positive. We’ll have to extend our graph to the left to include -3. If I plot the solution for x(0) = -3 in orange, I start over here at -3, over here. And I need to increase quickly, because f is very large at -3. So on the plot to the right, I’m moving to the right, and on the plot over here to the left I’m moving upward fast. But again as I approach negative 2, I slow down, and I get closer and closer to -2 as time increases. So just by looking at the graph of f(x), I can see what the behavior of the function should be for an initial condition -3. If x(0) is -3, I should increase to -2, but never quite get to -2.</p>
  
<p>One more case, what would happen if I chose the initial condition x(0) = 2.1? We’ll plot this in cyan, so if I start over here at 2.1, we can see that f(2.1) is positive. So on the graph to the left I start at 2.1, I need to start moving upward with time, but I move upward slowly because f is pretty small. I move to the right on the graph to the right, but as x gets to larger values, f(x) gets larger, so my speed increases, and x starts to increase faster and faster. And in fact, what happens is that x blows up as time increases.</p>
  
<p>The value x = 2 is another special point, because there f is 0, the velocity is 0, and so if I started at x = 2, x doesn’t change, I have a constant solution. So if my initial condition is 2, x(t) will be 2 for all time. The same thing was true for -2, and the purple line that we drew at first is actually a solution for the initial condition x(0) = -2.</p>
  
<p>These special solutions where x doesn’t change are called equilibria, and they can immediately be recognized from the graph of f, because they’re the points where f is 0. For our example differential equation, dx/dt = x^2 - 4, just by looking at the graph of f, we discovered that if the initial conditions are between -2 and 2, the solution will decay toward -2. If the initial condition is above 2, the solution will increase, and blow up. But if the initial condition is below -2, the function will increase and approach -2. So this is how we use the graph of the function to determine the behavior of the dynamical system or differential equation.
</p>
</section>
